## Building the Backend API with a Web Framework  

### 1. Real‑World Hook  
Imagine you’re a librarian who can instantly add a new bestseller, let patrons search for books by genre, and let users log in securely without ever touching a spreadsheet. That magic happens behind the scenes when a **RESTful backend API** powers your ebook management system. In this lesson we’ll turn that vision into code.

---

## 2. Choosing the Framework  

| Language | Popular Framework | Why It Fits the Course |
|----------|-------------------|------------------------|
| **JavaScript** | **Node.js + Express** | Lightweight, async‑first, easy to spin up REST endpoints. |
| **Python** | **Django REST Framework** | Batteries‑included, strong ORM, built‑in admin UI. |

> **Key Concept**  
> > *Pick a framework that aligns with your team’s skill set and the project’s scalability goals. For rapid prototyping, Express shines; for complex data relationships, Django offers a richer ecosystem.*  

---

## 3. Project Structure  

```
ebm-backend/
│
├─ src/
│   ├─ models/          # Data schemas (ORM mappings)
│   ├─ controllers/     # HTTP request handlers
│   ├─ services/        # Business logic
│   ├─ routes/          # URL routing
│   └─ middleware/      # Auth, validation, logging
│
├─ config/
│   └─ db.js            # Database connection
│
├─ .env                 # Environment variables
├─ package.json / requirements.txt
└─ server.js / wsgi.py  # Entry point
```

---

## 4. Setting Up the Database Layer  

### 4.1 Using an ORM (Sequelize for Node/Express)  

```js
// src/config/db.js
const { Sequelize } = require('sequelize');

const sequelize = new Sequelize(
  process.env.DB_NAME,
  process.env.DB_USER,
  process.env.DB_PASSWORD,
  {
    host: process.env.DB_HOST,
    dialect: 'postgres',
    logging: false,
  }
);

module.exports = sequelize;
```

### 4.2 Using Django’s Built‑in ORM  

```python
# config/db.py
import dj_database_url
from django.conf.global_settings import DATABASES

DATABASES['default'] = dj_database_url.config(
    default='postgres://user:password@localhost/dbname'
)
```

---

## 5. Defining Models  

### 5.1 Book Model (Sequelize)  

```js
// src/models/book.model.js
module.exports = (sequelize, DataTypes) => {
  const Book = sequelize.define('Book', {
    title:      { type: DataTypes.STRING, allowNull: false },
    author:     { type: DataTypes.STRING, allowNull: false },
    genre:      { type: DataTypes.STRING },
    isbn:       { type: DataTypes.STRING, unique: true },
    publishedAt:{ type: DataTypes.DATE },
  });
  return Book;
};
```

### 5.2 User & Token Model (Express)  

```js
// src/models/user.model.js
module.exports = (sequelize, DataTypes) => {
  const User = sequelize.define('User', {
    email:    { type: DataTypes.STRING, unique: true, allowNull: false },
    password: { type: DataTypes.STRING, allowNull: false },
  });
  return User;
};
```

---

## 6. Authentication (Hashed Passwords + JWT)  

### 6.1 Password Hashing  

```js
const bcrypt = require('bcrypt');
const SALT_ROUNDS = 10;

// Register endpoint snippet
app.post('/api/auth/register', async (req, res) => {
  const { email, password } = req.body;
  const hashed = await bcrypt.hash(password, SALT_ROUNDS);
  // save user with `hashed` password...
});
```

### 6.2 JWT Generation  

```js
const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');
const secret = process.env.JWT_SECRET;

const token = jwt.sign({ userId: user.id }, secret, { expiresIn: '1h' });
res.json({ token });
```

> **Key Concept**  
> > *Never store plain‑text passwords. Use a strong hashing algorithm (bcrypt/argon2) and protect JWTs with a secret stored outside the codebase (e.g., `.env`).*  

---

## 7. CRUD Endpoints for Book Inventory  

| HTTP Method | Endpoint               | Description                     |
|-------------|------------------------|---------------------------------|
| `POST`      | `/api/books`           | Create a new book               |
| `GET`       | `/api/books/:id`       | Retrieve a single book          |
| `GET`       | `/api/books`           | List all books (with filters)   |
| `PUT`       | `/api/books/:id`       | Update book details             |
| `DELETE`    | `/api/books/:id`       | Remove a book from inventory      |

### 7.1 Example: Create a Book  

```js
// src/controllers/book.controller.js
exports.createBook = async (req, res) => {
  try {
    const { title, author, genre, isbn } = req.body;
    const newBook = await Book.create({ title, author, genre, isbn });
    res.status(201).json(newBook);
  } catch (err) {
    res.status(400).json({ error: err.message });
  }
};
```

### 7.2 Example: Search & Filter  

```js
// src/controllers/book.controller.js
exports.searchBooks = async (req, res) => {
  const { title, author, genre } = req.query;
  const where = {};

  if (title)   where.title   = { [Op.iLike]: `%${title}%` };
  if (author)  where.author  = { [Op.iLike]: `%${author}%` };
  if (genre)   where.genre   = genre;

  const books = await Book.findAll({ where });
  res.json(books);
};
```

---

## 8. Routing & Controllers  

```js
// src/routes/book.routes.js
const router = require('express').Router();
const bookCtrl = require('../controllers/book.controller');

router.post('/', authMiddleware, bookCtrl.createBook);
router.get('/',  bookCtrl.searchBooks);
router.get('/:id', bookCtrl.getBook);
router.put('/:id', authMiddleware, bookCtrl.updateBook);
router.delete('/:id', authMiddleware, bookCtrl.deleteBook);

module.exports = router;
```

---

## 9. Putting It All Together  

```js
// server.js (entry point)
const express = require('express');
const sequelize = require('./config/db');
const bookRoutes = require('./routes/book.routes');
const authRoutes = require('./routes/auth.routes');

const app = express();
app.use(express.json());

sequelize.sync().then(() => console.log('DB connected'));

app.use('/api/auth', authRoutes);
app.use('/api/books', bookRoutes);

const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;
app.listen(PORT, () => console.log(`🚀 Server running on ${PORT}`));
```

---

## 10. Testing the API  

- **Unit tests** for services using Jest/Mocha.  
- **Integration tests** with SuperTest to hit real endpoints.  
- **Postman collection** or **cURL** commands for quick manual verification.

```bash
curl -X POST http://localhost:3000/api/books \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"title":"The Great Gatsby","author":"F. Scott Fitzgerald","genre":"Fiction","isbn":"9780743273565"}'
```

---

## Summary  

- **Hook**: A secure, searchable ebook backend transforms a static library into a dynamic service.  
- **Framework Choice**: Express for rapid REST APIs; Django REST for richer ORM needs.  
- **Structure**: Controllers, models, services, and routes keep code modular and maintainable.  
- **Data Layer**: Use an ORM (Sequelize/Django ORM) to map cleanly to your relational schema.  
- **Auth**: Hash passwords with bcrypt, issue JWTs for stateless sessions.  
- **CRUD & Search**: Implement endpoints that support creation, reading, updating, deletion, and flexible filtering.  
- **Testing**: Write unit and integration tests to ensure reliability before deployment.  

By following this roadmap, you’ll have a robust, production‑ready backend that powers the ebook management system’s front‑end and future extensions. Happy coding!

